[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234508 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d03866e6-9a0b-5659-acf4-038e7cb18b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-99273" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-\\frac{1}{6}$ causes $y=-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234509 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0550e664-f03a-58d5-a906-a1b13b8d0a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99333" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}}) & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234519 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7410dd72-8efd-59ee-929b-21eea426aeb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-99720" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}=\\frac{1}{{10}^{1/4}}={10}^{-1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234521 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b542da90-fa2b-563c-b1f0-c4fabe8e0315" :question/math-academy-id "q-9983" :question/problem "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $10-2+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-2}{10}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{5}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1+\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{6}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234523 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65ea4be7-7f1d-5940-92eb-a671bb136b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-99942" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a1ef1b-283f-5020-ba2d-b2f3346f1499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a1ef1b-283f-5020-ba2d-b2f3346f1499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234720} :answer-field/id #uuid "79216e8c-2905-5a27-aa0b-652b2135680c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df7cd8fe-37e2-547f-a82b-4acd638be632" :question/math-academy-id "q-128187" :question/problem "Use the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{2}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e7ba2c-f3da-5b13-acde-c1c4c8d12015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e7ba2c-f3da-5b13-acde-c1c4c8d12015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234743} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c3915d-9f67-5752-a50a-815c6237e44c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f69d44-046d-53d4-b5c4-befed46876e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82f69d44-046d-53d4-b5c4-befed46876e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234744} :answer-field/id #uuid "657ffe4f-24a7-5925-8cc2-cadb10650d2f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3eecfa1-cc73-5709-b7b5-4b5d14838f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3eecfa1-cc73-5709-b7b5-4b5d14838f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234877} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df74c9f-f142-51ad-ac12-61be12433bce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e503619d-21af-5f4b-b6eb-d5c17fa54595" :question/math-academy-id "q-128188" :question/problem "We wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $c=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28991446-ab1e-5a00-8794-50fc9d328a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93/16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28991446-ab1e-5a00-8794-50fc9d328a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93/16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234607} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b36cb32-3d96-57dd-a49e-b03269ddee44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17ab4fd5-a02b-55ea-bcd4-23bfc484ef65" :question/math-academy-id "q-128189" :question/problem "Use Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll start with the table from part 3.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now proceed to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using Euler's method.\n\n**Step 1.** We compute the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{45}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{45}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{45}{16} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+\\frac{45}{16}=\\frac{93}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{45}{4}$ | $\\frac{45}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{93}{16}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{93}{16}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8b9bdb-9838-55d0-bb49-abab662dbde1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{2}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b9bdb-9838-55d0-bb49-abab662dbde1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{2}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234769} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc37ec3a-8597-5c61-a04b-d9684c51116f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f9f3c49-fab7-5715-8827-fab0d52bd33d" :question/math-academy-id "q-128238" :question/problem "Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3{|}^{5}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1{|}^{3})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|]}_{-5}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{4}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{4}^{3}}{{2}^{5}\\cdot {3}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71cce186-30b6-55d0-84d4-342f5c753f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71cce186-30b6-55d0-84d4-342f5c753f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234716} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae52c6e-7b2f-5af5-8b96-5d84295d8e11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1e90155-6ac0-50f6-a4fb-8e3b6bbb20ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e90155-6ac0-50f6-a4fb-8e3b6bbb20ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234928} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6edf15-5a56-5db4-8849-32a5330ee399" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "333f44c6-e7aa-5f32-a7e4-9a58be270016" :question/math-academy-id "q-128339" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}=\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot 3+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=1$ and $B=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f49d51-a569-54b6-8499-614594a99518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(9/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2f49d51-a569-54b6-8499-614594a99518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(9/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234902} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb66c969-a116-55b7-83c4-7bdad75642f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "687b0f0d-b7fc-5c94-b602-200dc9ae2630" :question/math-academy-id "q-128340" :question/problem "${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cffe24-94be-52e7-a238-f2cdd9eed192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0cffe24-94be-52e7-a238-f2cdd9eed192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234843} :answer-field/id #uuid "9adce06b-fd56-5896-83e4-f0d2d7a527b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8c9449b-c7f6-57bf-a86b-68a3efa54686" :question/math-academy-id "q-128660" :question/problem "Consider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "In part 2 we found that ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{21}{25}.$ Also,\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{7}{2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3}=-\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5-25f(x)$ and $v=g(x)+3.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot (g(x)+3)-(5-25f(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x)+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot (g(2)+3)-(5-25f(2))\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2)+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25\\cdot (-\\frac{21}{25})\\cdot (-2+3)-(5-25\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{(-2+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =21-20 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8185b83-eb29-58df-bdeb-f269a4c2146c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6-12{t}^{2})/(2{t}^{3}+3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8185b83-eb29-58df-bdeb-f269a4c2146c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6-12{t}^{2})/(2{t}^{3}+3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234829} :answer-field/id #uuid "719e6a79-1ce1-54b1-8c76-98d46b85de7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "632a77f1-9681-5dcd-aa11-3a8387a03565" :question/math-academy-id "q-132978" :question/problem "The particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot 2{(\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc58bfdf-5c61-564b-9179-b99d997e008a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{66}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc58bfdf-5c61-564b-9179-b99d997e008a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{66}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234834} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd67718-6c2e-5e11-a160-7539ce5a4737" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76551198-3b30-5f90-a773-8f46f7e7ea21" :question/math-academy-id "q-132980" :question/problem "The acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-24t)(2{t}^{3}+3t)-(6-12{t}^{2})(6{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-48{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-36{t}^{2}-18+72{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-18}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(4{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}-3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{6(4(1)^{4}-12(1)^{2}-3)}{(2(1)^{3}+3(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-11)}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{66}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{66}{25}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f0c4bf-c884-5ffe-9bbb-fd66e54891c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f0c4bf-c884-5ffe-9bbb-fd66e54891c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19367e3-97ce-5783-bf46-3e1a0dadc9a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c85a07f-1862-53e2-9986-87c4ee966897" :question/math-academy-id "q-132983" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}})+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d974e101-1852-5cc5-a2c2-e4157e1121a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d974e101-1852-5cc5-a2c2-e4157e1121a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234887} :answer-field/id #uuid "079df57d-3a7f-5abb-9ebe-062f08636271" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4701d5c-31ce-52fa-8163-7497d5963cba" :question/math-academy-id "q-139445" :question/problem "Expressing the answer as a fraction, Maria's expected profit on her next turn is$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         dollars." :question/worked-solution "From part 5, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n\nThe expected value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =-6\\cdot f(-6)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =-6(\\frac{1}{3})+(-2)(\\frac{1}{3})+6(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-2-\\frac{2}{3}+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-\\frac{2}{3}$ dollars."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8af484f-e0a3-50cf-8367-274f5910242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.137" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8af484f-e0a3-50cf-8367-274f5910242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.137" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234912} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed6fe7ac-7dc7-519a-8260-f54be485f15a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ffa4be7-fd0e-5296-b565-824e840e9738" :question/math-academy-id "q-139453" :question/problem "The money in the saving box accumulates until the very last game. Rounded to three decimal places, the probability that the saving box contains exactly $$20$ after $10$ games is$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "In each drawn game, both players deposit $$2$ each, or $$4$ in total, into the saving box with probability $\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nHence, the saving box will contain $$20$ dollars if Maria and John draw $5$ games.\n\nThen, if $Y$ is the number of times a game is drawn out of $10$ possible games, then \n\n$$\nY∼B(10,\\frac{1}{3}).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(y)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{y}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{y}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{10-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{5}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{10-5} \\\\  & =252{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5} \\\\  & =0.137,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f35e2d-66b6-54fe-a062-977604208172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f35e2d-66b6-54fe-a062-977604208172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234827} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d44031-6cdd-5336-a8be-754448adef36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fce5f29-50bd-5618-8615-2260116a577f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166287" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8be44b9-b42d-52ce-90b8-6bab2b790332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +2h)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8be44b9-b42d-52ce-90b8-6bab2b790332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +2h)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234913} :answer-field/id #uuid "fff82516-d651-5957-98ab-d8adc8fad4dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9588cc2-fb5a-5a22-93e8-5666e235065a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166289" :question/problem "What is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h)-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h))-5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5sin⁡(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16242856-ba0f-5959-96a9-cc84fceb8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{8{sin}^{2}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16242856-ba0f-5959-96a9-cc84fceb8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{8{sin}^{2}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234573} :answer-field/id #uuid "7da92529-d318-5dfa-bfa7-0df43fae7490" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c7fd239-c698-5700-aea8-d260b2cef38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167328" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6387390a-abb2-5dfb-b224-6f303cebe5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6387390a-abb2-5dfb-b224-6f303cebe5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234698} :answer-field/id #uuid "03f33ffc-fcf9-5c5d-8bb8-d042e1b2ae97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab911cf6-6e60-568c-ba99-7878f0275992" :question/math-academy-id "q-167558" :question/problem "![](images/37/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=5+\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234897} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa3c58-8fd9-5d31-988a-048384e9e06f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cee13c2e-6226-5f1c-9ea2-cd4611314d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-167564" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{(\\frac{6}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b95d6c4-5d7b-5dc8-88d7-f9b4485ad8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b95d6c4-5d7b-5dc8-88d7-f9b4485ad8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234590} :answer-field/id #uuid "7988cab9-4497-5a8a-841c-22ed9c51d002" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d280c885-e199-544c-b66d-467329b4f2c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167566" :question/problem "Find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=2{(-2)}^{2}=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe4d5118-310c-52c1-8792-82278a9c6243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe4d5118-310c-52c1-8792-82278a9c6243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234945} :answer-field/id #uuid "2030a9e3-3cc4-5c91-8889-5080c5638f5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfb96854-7123-5e58-a1c9-faaa3c019dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-167570" :question/problem "Another particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function ${v}_{2}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})=(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})=(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d0fb2c-78da-56c6-8581-f4b20ffce30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d0fb2c-78da-56c6-8581-f4b20ffce30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234675} :answer-field/id #uuid "83d6963f-f877-507f-bf95-e4d5ac3e236d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8491154-cb55-56d4-b408-9058d547cc94" :question/math-academy-id "q-167571" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d0654e0-d49d-534f-9b33-68e3e971d9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(t/2)-t/2\\operatorname{sin}(t/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d0654e0-d49d-534f-9b33-68e3e971d9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(t/2)-t/2\\operatorname{sin}(t/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234758} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdafbc71-e376-54dc-999f-eac8f84227b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dd0c3db-6967-5a89-a656-a9043eef1f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-167572" :question/problem "Find the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)=t$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=1,\\,{h}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))+(t)(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f42bdd68-d7ff-53db-b3b0-bdc3bed81956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f42bdd68-d7ff-53db-b3b0-bdc3bed81956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234932} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b42b4d1-0b65-5db6-846b-bc92dccc887b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ced32af-bad9-5a14-a058-555b5adb8c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-167634" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We take the constant factor out of the integral and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{(-2+1)}}{-2+1})+C \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1})+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8001348-dd53-534f-8d2b-dd7335396166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8001348-dd53-534f-8d2b-dd7335396166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234860} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fd67b44-4d31-5a37-a044-5583e68fcdce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd371490-b54b-5976-bf71-68a2fedb858d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167651" :question/problem "Write down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ae6381-27b6-5f93-8131-98453b145862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32ae6381-27b6-5f93-8131-98453b145862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234625} :answer-field/id #uuid "90dbff55-8f3a-5c54-81ca-c8b7d5227587" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4ea7df7-6856-52ff-9d9c-29beb9f68c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167782" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})=\\frac{e^x}{8\\sin^2(x)}\\bigl(${{field-1}}$\\bigr)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9d00ec8-e865-5151-9a4c-bad4652bfb11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9d00ec8-e865-5151-9a4c-bad4652bfb11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234805} :answer-field/id #uuid "079110b6-6c4b-5d0b-9a1e-a17faca83de8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f543150-38e4-557e-9ab8-57056b911cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167938" :question/problem "Construct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a00763ad-4e74-5366-b4cf-96d7c6960003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00763ad-4e74-5366-b4cf-96d7c6960003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234789} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb3200af-b225-5fa1-908f-fd20b7ba66ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c54e8fe-499b-5467-a47e-09bd98bc5054" :question/math-academy-id "q-167939" :question/problem "The average distance from the origin to a point on the circumference of the circle is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Since $r(\\theta )$ represents the distance of each point on the circle from the origin, the average distance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6-(-6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa232cfc-645e-5d26-8f7a-e64c1ba82e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa232cfc-645e-5d26-8f7a-e64c1ba82e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234941} :answer-field/id #uuid "232c8eb8-e105-5135-97a7-de91270cf6ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5608163c-bf57-5039-b653-5bdcdbba7103" :question/math-academy-id "q-168036" :question/problem "Find the angle $\\theta $ between vectors $q$ and $r.$ Round your answer to the nearest degree.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\theta =$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the angle $\\theta $ between the vectors, we can rearrange the dot product formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q\\cdot r & =|\\,q\\,|\\cdot |\\,r\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{q\\cdot r}{|\\,q\\,||\\,r\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom part 4, we have $|\\,q\\,|=\\sqrt{13}$ and $|\\,r\\,|=5.$ Also, from part 5, we have $q\\cdot r=-6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{q\\cdot r}{|\\,q\\,||\\,r\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{\\sqrt{13}\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{13}})≈{109}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f4b1b0a-72e5-51b7-976a-a11cf6167471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0009(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4b1b0a-72e5-51b7-976a-a11cf6167471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0009(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234768} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8df629-31a4-5279-82d1-b3a16b07e686" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a44df9e2-3cc1-515c-b385-288559e72721" :question/math-academy-id "q-168076" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-0.03(T-25)) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-0.03T+0.75) \\\\  & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(T)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0.75) \\\\  & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ into the above and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =-0.03(-0.03(T-25)) \\\\  & =0.0009(T-25)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e22b95ce-19c7-597b-82d6-f2f0a8f2c349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22b95ce-19c7-597b-82d6-f2f0a8f2c349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234899} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c22f74-5292-5666-a98a-16bc52666d98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41502521-84c6-55a9-b25a-df8d2f944731" :question/math-academy-id "q-168606" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a thin metal plate that occupies the region $D$ defined in part 6 and has charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x+y.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $Q=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the region $D$ represents a quarter-annulus enclosed between the circles of the radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x+y$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn part 6, by transforming to polar coordinates, we found that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0))\\cdot (9-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the total charge of the object is $Q=\\frac{52}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e443a3d-ee4f-5d48-878b-3796be7fc0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k)/6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e443a3d-ee4f-5d48-878b-3796be7fc0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k)/6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234689} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d25038b-8b0c-528f-a9f2-5006c6c60637" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cbe55c0-4132-5bbd-88da-51f994b615f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170575" :question/problem "When Amy's knight reaches level $k,$ he has $k+2$ swords. However, a knight can only use $3$ swords each day. \n\nFind a **polynomial** expression, in terms of $k$, for the number of ways Amy can use her swords on a particular day when at level $k.$ You may assume that the order in which the swords are used does not matter.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The number of combinations equals \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Since the order in which the swords are used does not matter, the number of ways the swords can be used is a combination of the available swords.\n\nThe number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nWhen at level $k,$ there are $k+2$ swords, so $n=k+2.$ We want to select $3$ of those, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different selections of three swords is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{k+2}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{(k+2)!}{((k+2)-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot (\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 5, we found that $\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}={k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{k+2}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot (\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}({k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c304fc9-c9da-5d97-9759-ccf6e138d12f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236194} {:answer/id #uuid "53809d10-dcef-5e85-886e-cc325cd763be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236347} {:answer/id #uuid "77c7b91a-bbc1-589a-884f-c42b2bb99fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236569} {:answer/id #uuid "78657ffb-16ea-5821-a776-4f1b2747a9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236573} {:answer/id #uuid "c385379d-8237-5252-97dc-f26a22711a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53809d10-dcef-5e85-886e-cc325cd763be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236347} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6e6951-f040-51c5-a0b5-e70540d8f884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc8a355a-ee17-5da1-9a5f-fc0c8e06bcf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-10009" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=\\pi /2} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482b02ae-d24d-54b9-bec3-9abb19ad3a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236281} {:answer/id #uuid "5109d123-8dc4-51ab-ab40-bc9ecd7d988e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236334} {:answer/id #uuid "94ecd205-8acf-5dfb-8b92-028d364421e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236761} {:answer/id #uuid "a7056fd7-4693-5e50-92d2-77473a227f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236863} {:answer/id #uuid "f1cbddf2-0764-540e-8959-a9fe3253a384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5109d123-8dc4-51ab-ab40-bc9ecd7d988e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236334} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b561d1f-b212-5afc-9626-cc8d7995672a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39d52e4f-2b82-55ff-af5e-28f93f87917e" :question/math-academy-id "q-100797" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}=13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x} & =13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}-13x\\cdot {8}^{x}-6\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-13x-6)\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(5x+2)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3.$\n\n- The equation ${8}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${8}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot 3=-\\frac{6}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0334c69f-0452-5acd-a57c-9462cf654e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{94.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235815} {:answer/id #uuid "29495e5b-4e96-525b-8fc6-026cb700d48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236076} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef32d15-14d0-5120-a3fd-76117b97e353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{89.9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236115} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d8ee57-ff29-5cc1-ba37-b1af4502a79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{91.7}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237198} {:answer/id #uuid "e357153b-3ba1-5f08-8a65-540358428351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{102.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0334c69f-0452-5acd-a57c-9462cf654e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{94.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235815} :answer-field/id #uuid "59cbab81-5ee1-5448-a448-4516adfa6a97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c0bf9dc-454a-56a5-bcdd-445536550323" :question/math-academy-id "q-101501" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+(-6)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{37}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{629}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{629}})≈{94.6}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a2c7bc9-1f70-5c7f-81e3-8c278f707e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236583} {:answer/id #uuid "7be243cd-bfa9-5a06-bf89-4a335a131769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236590} {:answer/id #uuid "a38c0758-6f04-50ea-906c-462af713bb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{69.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236844} {:answer/id #uuid "dd234bf9-ebef-597b-9e5e-86a5a467cf93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{71.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237210} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b789be-2da3-5505-a0c2-323f58c7f107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{74.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b789be-2da3-5505-a0c2-323f58c7f107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{74.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237356} :answer-field/id #uuid "330a4c79-879b-50ab-82f5-138b2b350866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e426e37-8c07-5ee5-8ef8-255e5e023aff" :question/math-academy-id "q-101502" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=⟨7,2⟩$ and $b=⟨0,6⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{53}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{53}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{53}})≈{74.1}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175607d2-4c72-5207-ae52-84db44b45b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84.37}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235963} {:answer/id #uuid "2b2b442a-454f-5262-8bf4-a74349db9040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236087} {:answer/id #uuid "5f53bd9b-7010-5890-81bd-c0a4f29a6dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{71.25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236418} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1f5cdd-3176-5a36-9b54-8d99dcd2a0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{69.56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236699} {:answer/id #uuid "ac170887-ceae-5b84-8699-110ba9cf51ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac170887-ceae-5b84-8699-110ba9cf51ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236908} :answer-field/id #uuid "7209ce86-f415-54bf-be6a-bef839c2edfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36bc5a94-57a6-56e7-857e-178854ad126d" :question/math-academy-id "q-101504" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{546}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{546}})≈{75.12}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a8e4a99-a9c5-5fc7-9f78-396522052d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236185} {:answer/id #uuid "586c1b66-72bd-5e5a-809f-ce7b04846d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236374} {:answer/id #uuid "a9efda7a-7dc8-56d3-b172-0ab18dcb5157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.09}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236887} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0743c8-027e-5f13-a875-f054dcc036c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77.11}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237112} {:answer/id #uuid "e813f475-9a7c-503d-b72f-d583e6e956a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{78.56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a8e4a99-a9c5-5fc7-9f78-396522052d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236185} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6595c31-e9b8-5500-bed2-a7809530f6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bb24474-02b6-5a79-8993-e3afc25833c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-101505" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 6+6\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{100}\\cdot \\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{10\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{6}{35})≈{80.13}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038e0307-c334-55eb-bdf7-692cdb33f023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235819} {:answer/id #uuid "1b828b15-9546-5cf0-85ab-65052d538e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235988} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4453d6-d8fd-5271-b0b4-bfe00f1aacbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236116} {:answer/id #uuid "a55c473c-9e6a-53ad-a6d6-0755e21624d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236856} {:answer/id #uuid "af068867-e87c-56b9-9198-3ae81095df86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4453d6-d8fd-5271-b0b4-bfe00f1aacbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236116} :answer-field/id #uuid "a361fc99-c6c3-5763-b077-11d54bd19f13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4443b852-41a5-5a76-994c-081bd2938800" :question/math-academy-id "q-101506" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(1,5,-1)$, $Q(5,1,1)$, and $R(2,5,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨1,5,-1⟩,\\,q=⟨5,1,1⟩,\\,r=⟨2,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,5,0⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a9d790-8c79-5157-b653-7986519a7244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235849} {:answer/id #uuid "09c5cbed-6f4e-5c5b-b8d4-222ddd693831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235863} {:answer/id #uuid "606cf9a3-3f7c-56b0-9b40-02626d8c46b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236431} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd80225-443c-5987-aa15-bc96869c1f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236499} {:answer/id #uuid "886c9fa5-5715-5ba8-bd74-2ec7a617fac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07a9d790-8c79-5157-b653-7986519a7244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235849} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef88b2e4-dee4-549f-8993-e598ab808c21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6125d20d-d091-5df9-9c87-5b9a645bb5dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-101507" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(3,3,-5)$, $Q(4,2,-3)$, and $R(7,5,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨3,3,-5⟩,\\,q=⟨4,2,-3⟩,\\,r=⟨7,5,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-3⟩-⟨3,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨7,5,-3⟩-⟨3,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb800d1-316e-514c-a314-b17fd4819266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236308} {:answer/id #uuid "560f9469-01dd-562c-b3d6-98f5568759cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236364} {:answer/id #uuid "60e9bf56-4bab-56a6-93ae-8d5a924e7b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236434} {:answer/id #uuid "e787eddd-d597-5585-89fa-c870f97eb1a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237272} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c11f96-d160-5a39-bb26-55336eac5252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cb800d1-316e-514c-a314-b17fd4819266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236308} :answer-field/id #uuid "818362db-3bf1-5219-abfe-548221ff3abe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "338863d2-2756-57cd-a7ee-a62638ca5a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-101509" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(-5,1,3)$, $Q(-3,-1,2)$, and $R(-5,2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨-5,1,3⟩,\\,q=⟨-3,-1,2⟩,\\,r=⟨-5,2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,2⟩-⟨-5,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨-5,2,4⟩-⟨-5,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={135}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a3c3ca-de21-5a89-976a-74df76fee0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235915} {:answer/id #uuid "430d0ec7-692f-5566-ba01-38e14e164229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236248} {:answer/id #uuid "674f4285-d4b8-5544-88db-cb1db07a1732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236468} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b9b872-cd09-5db9-addb-15180a2cf28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237140} {:answer/id #uuid "f85d71c9-68c6-5490-ad0a-661010e6535b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{91}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "674f4285-d4b8-5544-88db-cb1db07a1732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236468} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce4fb03-3a62-573f-992d-81083e58ad47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5610dd8-caf7-5583-bce9-9599524f1903" :question/math-academy-id "q-101511" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,1)$, $B(3,1,-2)$, and $C(-1,1,1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,b=⟨3,1,-2⟩,\\,c=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{182}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{182}})≈{86}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "279dbfad-79dc-50f6-b6fa-3ce7edf1161a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236066} {:answer/id #uuid "630a3920-ccb0-5a60-89f2-30f6fbcc0e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236444} {:answer/id #uuid "681203af-d4b1-51c3-94d0-513c563723ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236475} {:answer/id #uuid "df5de9da-72ad-5de5-a4ce-79bea1997639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237221} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f6a6af-7ef5-5986-8844-e994ff9f43c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df5de9da-72ad-5de5-a4ce-79bea1997639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237221} :answer-field/id #uuid "d525463c-b43a-5061-b33f-8f38a0b0c0cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "894928dd-75e2-52ce-a7a7-400cde0a1a19" :question/math-academy-id "q-101512" :question/problem "Given that $a\\cdot b=-5$, find the angle between $a=⟨2t,t⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $a\\cdot b=-5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-5 \\\\ 2t\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 3 & =-5 \\\\ 2t+3t & =-5 \\\\ 5t & =-5 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨2(-1),\\,(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $a$ and $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={135}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b127c2d-8183-52ec-aa42-8fba09e84b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{111.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236188} {:answer/id #uuid "45a4b336-b028-5a04-a1a7-9c78406f7c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{113.8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236265} {:answer/id #uuid "6282d327-9bd2-5cc8-8b36-2c0e0a56ecda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{116.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236441} {:answer/id #uuid "da06971c-de78-57e8-94eb-4a059d4488cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{122.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237199} {:answer/id #uuid "f7674b52-b979-533c-a030-12bbf0eed2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120.9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6282d327-9bd2-5cc8-8b36-2c0e0a56ecda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{116.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236441} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c90838-220b-5fd5-ad49-396886fdd86d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c596d00-60e5-5ca2-b29c-a3daf98db598" :question/math-academy-id "q-101514" :question/problem "Given that $a\\cdot b=-10$, find the angle between $a=⟨-4t,3t⟩$ and $b=⟨2,1⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since $a\\cdot b=-10$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-10 \\\\ (-4t)\\cdot 2+3t\\cdot 1 & =-10 \\\\ -8t+3t & =-10 \\\\ -5t & =-10 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨-4(2),\\,3(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $a$ and $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{10\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})≈{116.6}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04877c92-80b7-5772-8ae0-bb4712f99050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235827} {:answer/id #uuid "711fd593-b3ca-5bff-8f14-1dbd7e25e8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{39.9}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236537} {:answer/id #uuid "a33eff44-5a98-51cd-a282-cca703c44454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236840} {:answer/id #uuid "aeda944b-e1b7-520a-9340-c18b09e05a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236931} {:answer/id #uuid "f66882a0-fe93-511f-8d59-0c450cfaee5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aeda944b-e1b7-520a-9340-c18b09e05a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236931} :answer-field/id #uuid "6315cc58-4dfc-5fa4-b54d-b010430c620e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e81441db-c7f8-5399-a086-f63e36d0d974" :question/math-academy-id "q-101515" :question/problem "Given that $p\\cdot q=11$, find the angle between $p=⟨t,t-1⟩$ and $q=⟨-4,-1⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since $p\\cdot q=11$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =11 \\\\ t\\cdot (-4)+(t-1)\\cdot (-1) & =11 \\\\ -4t-t+1 & =11 \\\\ -5t & =10 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨(-2),(-2)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $p$ and $q$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,p\\,||\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{13}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{221}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{221}})≈{42.3}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10235f9e-72ee-5e8a-a405-509459ae38e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235901} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7fc012-7fa0-5c62-8fcc-c1948c9090a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{73.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236397} {:answer/id #uuid "63841e36-f180-55db-a1fd-2610cc481f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{71.36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236447} {:answer/id #uuid "97c478ce-2c26-538b-8944-b7f6d7cacd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.84}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236779} {:answer/id #uuid "eea589cb-56d1-5155-b2d8-f851fdc96520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eea589cb-56d1-5155-b2d8-f851fdc96520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237320} :answer-field/id #uuid "30c81564-ebbf-55c9-8c75-898784e645a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c492ca5-356b-5447-a4a2-dead0bbc3177" :question/math-academy-id "q-101517" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(2t,-1,5)$, $B(-3,t,1)$ and $C(2t+1,2,-t).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩,\\,b=⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩,\\,c=⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,t,\\,1⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3-2t,\\,t+1,\\,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2t+1,\\,2,\\,-t⟩-⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3,\\,-t-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =5 \\\\ (-3-2t)\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\cdot 3+-4\\cdot (-t-5) & =5 \\\\ -3-2t+3t+3+4t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t+20 & =5 \\\\ 5t & =-15 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-3-2(-3),(-3)+1,-4⟩, \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-4⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =1,\\,3,\\,-(-3)-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{406}})≈{75.63}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49cf810b-88d7-566b-98a2-2877abaad8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{70.99}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236291} {:answer/id #uuid "694f53ee-cdbb-540d-99ca-5c4af429e0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{78.55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236483} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c89ac2-12f3-5dee-aca1-edf867a55912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.22}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236866} {:answer/id #uuid "aed5880c-80bd-52d5-9dd2-b807e070979d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80.44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236930} {:answer/id #uuid "fda4c203-9ac0-561e-aab1-e6e2fadcb901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.33}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aed5880c-80bd-52d5-9dd2-b807e070979d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80.44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236930} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d9e7d1a-754a-533e-a451-15cc99b64951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f794a26e-b5b3-578d-ad06-15f9b4b4e601" :question/math-academy-id "q-101520" :question/problem "Consider the points $D(t,5,3)$, $E({t}^{2},2,t)$ and $F(t+3,{t}^{2},1).$ Find $∠EDF$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}=6.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $D$, $E$, $F$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=⟨t,\\,5,\\,3⟩,\\,e=⟨{t}^{2},\\,2,\\,t⟩,\\,f=⟨t+3,\\,{t}^{2},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△DEF$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠EDF=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/c9/c9cfb3e84e6f76d243125f2a09246b2a1300a0134e71359bb2b528082f064f93.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,2,\\,t⟩-⟨t,\\,5,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,\\,-3,\\,t-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =⟨t+3,\\,{t}^{2},\\,1⟩-⟨t,\\,5,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,{t}^{2}-5,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}=6$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =6 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot ({t}^{2}-5)+(t-3)\\cdot (-2) & =6 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-3t-3{t}^{2}+15-2t+6 & =6 \\\\ -5t+21 & =6 \\\\ -5t & =-15 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨{3}^{2}-3,-3,3-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,-3,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨3,{3}^{2}-5,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,4,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}}{|\\overset{\\to }{DE}||\\overset{\\to }{DF}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{145}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{145}})≈{80.44}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae7ead7-181b-5833-a116-31f0377cd8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236084} {:answer/id #uuid "70b2c173-e023-59b4-bf4f-792730e93db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236532} {:answer/id #uuid "70c2cf83-482d-5dae-9db6-502db0b85ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236533} {:answer/id #uuid "84dcc40c-1ac0-5618-9182-fd3c12380aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236642} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a8596c-db12-5375-bbc6-35c46196b6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70c2cf83-482d-5dae-9db6-502db0b85ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236533} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8d9514-9eda-561e-b725-77622b71dc92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da039a17-2a9d-5f24-9d07-024eac6133bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-101522" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=14$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0384a71b-bf28-515f-8b2b-476abb5fca12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-10+7{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235817} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8c24ce-0ee5-5eba-b662-bb6dcb3d78e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-10+21{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236321} {:answer/id #uuid "5659c514-e496-58f4-9efc-5892bb18fbec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}-10+21{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236366} {:answer/id #uuid "682d240f-295a-54b0-8f99-5ef92b911aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+10+21{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236477} {:answer/id #uuid "fee011ed-5418-5bfa-a0ce-9610f711b134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x}+10+21{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5659c514-e496-58f4-9efc-5892bb18fbec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}-10+21{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236366} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a9552c-cb8c-5d34-a069-fe4c1cccbba6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ed4b1bb-20cc-53c5-859e-2495715a907d" :question/math-academy-id "q-101716" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-10x+7{x}^{3}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-10x+7{x}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{x})-10+21{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-10+21{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b32625-e9e5-563b-839b-42adeb20be73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{11}{2},1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236242} {:answer/id #uuid "4df95115-ebe6-57f1-95ca-f41c99471efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{9}{2},1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236315} {:answer/id #uuid "5f06f67c-d421-5bdd-a544-bd3b46e1f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{9}{2},2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236415} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbe9c28-bf2a-5e90-8453-b0d39a6514ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236596} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ade3ad-b14b-5699-bf42-c67e2fd932b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42b32625-e9e5-563b-839b-42adeb20be73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{11}{2},1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236242} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4de894-66e9-5d27-878e-0a6e914b403a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "498c3ac6-7680-54fc-be56-c8179953d99f" :question/math-academy-id "q-10197" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y-z &=4 \\\\ y+2z &=2 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "he third equation states that  $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(\\frac{1}{2})=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(1)-(\\frac{1}{2})=4\\,⟹\\,x=4+1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{11}{2}$,  $y=1$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨\\frac{11}{2},1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0947bcb2-124b-5a9b-8c2d-caaf1dd4d78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,-3,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235859} {:answer/id #uuid "4b19167d-8908-5eb5-b1b2-35217d6c79d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨9,-3,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236299} {:answer/id #uuid "6b66fc33-4c80-5b0f-8c9f-716a423a5b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨11,3,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236494} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0a4fc5-1033-5109-8969-8d7ba972a0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨9,2,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236922} {:answer/id #uuid "d095b16d-d82d-5f43-b45a-419f5d3af373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨11,3,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b66fc33-4c80-5b0f-8c9f-716a423a5b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨11,3,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236494} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1426b6-aff3-5cb0-a4a0-23caf436597b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcc22116-bc14-5a39-b8c2-56ca9a77cd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-10198" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=9 \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-3(\\frac{1}{3})=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(3)+3(\\frac{1}{3})=9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9+3-1=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨11,3,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063d9066-2710-5baa-9457-1f82db646578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(x)=\\sqrt[5]{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235839} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc85329-e98d-5e73-95c4-6f2b329d8753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236502} {:answer/id #uuid "79f88c67-1f14-5149-8ebf-212135622bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & x<-1 \\\\ x-1,\\, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236582} {:answer/id #uuid "a52a319a-dfa3-5bd8-934f-8cfe0a6f5e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236854} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a284d6-cd28-5c06-96ad-d15fb4f0a65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a284d6-cd28-5c06-96ad-d15fb4f0a65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237196} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7297bbd-9da3-5e63-8ac7-2c2ae42656d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4fad11de-5b75-50e6-bc02-1a3c1209884c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102159" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $h(x)$ is differentiable for all $x\\ne 1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140ff256-58cb-520e-bd9f-ea7c8a886af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235938} {:answer/id #uuid "1b82390d-5224-5735-9b05-52d62582a7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235987} {:answer/id #uuid "42c2e22e-7c41-5aba-866b-c2bfb66a3484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236246} {:answer/id #uuid "76520e43-a300-5719-9281-b069c04cc15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236558} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7c3eb2-612e-5bea-9351-f3a4af8fd436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "140ff256-58cb-520e-bd9f-ea7c8a886af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235938} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbea9dd8-14a2-537f-a32b-13a92d5ed3d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec04a0d1-192f-5dcf-aa4b-20bf3669c6e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-102192" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{(-\\frac{3}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eebe26b-56cf-593b-a183-01df97f38f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236114} {:answer/id #uuid "393b4ae7-8737-50f6-85bd-d17aa46ba699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236180} {:answer/id #uuid "462092c8-6ce0-5a48-82ca-8e16a547d371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236270} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc4a6d9-bc67-54c7-bcc2-79d4c122b050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236331} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d1d38-b7ff-5edd-b964-2849a17db8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e39d1d38-b7ff-5edd-b964-2849a17db8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237250} :answer-field/id #uuid "6933f143-14c1-564f-887f-fb1687c35a07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b161241-4160-51dc-b8ef-c729dd1d8482" :question/math-academy-id "q-102337" :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-4)+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{(-4)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-8}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}=5\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5})=-7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e65dd22-0c47-5b8f-80fe-8a35924d61cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)}+\\frac{5}{(3-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236013} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2b27c4-3f08-56f8-9ee8-a28e9e4040b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}+\\frac{2}{(3-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236493} {:answer/id #uuid "8dac7305-ee26-55c0-96b1-d829bc4316c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)}+\\frac{5}{(3-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236713} {:answer/id #uuid "91664327-90af-56d6-941f-24de5b719221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)}+\\frac{4}{(3-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236740} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcbe96b-9f18-5614-bb0d-a7a8be519329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{7}{(x-4)}-\\frac{3}{(3-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b2b27c4-3f08-56f8-9ee8-a28e9e4040b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}+\\frac{2}{(3-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236493} :answer-field/id #uuid "05599ffd-565f-5675-9ce1-bcbfc4aedb1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48525aef-1401-5350-b706-3dd8e85f6bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-102531" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{37-19x}{(3x+1)(x-4)(3-x)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37-19x}{(3x+1)(x-4)(3-x)} & =\\frac{A}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)}+\\frac{C}{(3-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(3x+1)(x-4)(3-x)$ gives\n\n$$\n37-19x=A(x-4)(3-x)+B(3x+1)(3-x)+C(3x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37-19(-\\frac{1}{3}) & =A(-\\frac{1}{3}-4)(3-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & +B(3(-\\frac{1}{3})+1)(3-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & +C(3(-\\frac{1}{3})+1)(-\\frac{1}{3}-4) \\\\ 37+\\frac{19}{3} & =A(-\\frac{130}{9}) \\\\ \\frac{130}{3} & =A(-\\frac{130}{9}) \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=4$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37-19(4) & =A(4-4)(3-4) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(4)+1)(3-4) \\\\  & +C(3(4)+1)(x-4) \\\\ 37-76 & =B(-13) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-39}{-13} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37-19(3) & =A(3-4)(3-3) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(3)+1)(3-3) \\\\  & +C(3(3)+1)(3-4) \\\\ 37-57 & =C(-10) \\\\ C & =\\frac{-20}{-10} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{37-19x}{(3x+1)(x-4)(3-x)}=-\\frac{3}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}+\\frac{2}{(3-x)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7c16b9-ca47-5bdc-8afb-fb24b5d329e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235865} {:answer/id #uuid "1572b304-d016-515b-a00b-811e20872dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235946} {:answer/id #uuid "2d036771-7cf5-5a43-9518-e68dcfb124b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236100} {:answer/id #uuid "91d30ab3-a94e-50b0-a6af-f7fe19ea56d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236741} {:answer/id #uuid "fd24b45f-3cdb-531f-8617-5b313bb90baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd24b45f-3cdb-531f-8617-5b313bb90baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237422} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc20b91-0a45-5c2a-b7bf-fc2cf43de006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e760206-a007-5062-8f2d-fefff458b4ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-102552" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}+20x+3}{(3x+2)(x-1)(x+5)}=\\frac{A}{(3x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, calculate $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}+20x+3}{(3x+2)(x-1)(x+5)}=\\frac{A}{(3x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(3x+2)(x-1)(x+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}+20x+3=A(x-1)(x+5)+B(3x+2)(x+5)+C(3x+2)(x-1).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+20(-\\frac{2}{3})+3 & =A(-\\frac{2}{3}-1)(-\\frac{2}{3}+5) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)(-\\frac{2}{3}+5) \\\\  & \\,+C(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)(-\\frac{2}{3}-1) \\\\ \\frac{28}{9}-\\frac{40}{3}+3 & =A(-\\frac{65}{9}) \\\\ -\\frac{65}{9} & =A(-\\frac{65}{9}) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)^{2}+20(1)+3 & =A(1-1)(1+5) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(1)+2)(1+5) \\\\  & \\,+C(3(1)+2)(1-1) \\\\ 7+20+3 & =B(30) \\\\ B & =\\frac{30}{30} \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-5)^{2}+20(-5)+3 & =A(-5-1)(-5+5) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(-5)+2)(-5+5) \\\\  & \\,+C(3(-5)+2)(-5-1) \\\\ 175-100+3 & =C(78) \\\\ C & =\\frac{78}{78} \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+C=1+1+1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241e58ea-86ce-5450-939e-02de03cdebfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236050} {:answer/id #uuid "2e56d505-7726-5af1-871c-95766070a7e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236112} {:answer/id #uuid "5b86c06b-4025-5e46-b183-b3a382a369a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236390} {:answer/id #uuid "62be5c5d-404d-5b63-b96a-01802a0419b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236442} {:answer/id #uuid "f134bbd3-d5b3-51e4-9905-9b65f60e5fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241e58ea-86ce-5450-939e-02de03cdebfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236050} :answer-field/id #uuid "86665b82-4f5e-59d4-b139-f213cd90fc89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeda4143-b348-582e-8960-ff5a4ad7dde2" :question/math-academy-id "q-10256" :question/problem "![](images/da/da529910e268ea4c124d7c27b085ca1799e255604459c539cc8d5809dda8f95a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a98e176-de94-501d-9654-ee79fa546361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235866} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbcf497-35c1-530e-8b8a-1e4520aff5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236401} {:answer/id #uuid "64a92041-6332-5b56-9a49-329279ae7898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236454} {:answer/id #uuid "c676c67b-ce2e-5ee3-a924-56c7b533c894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237076} {:answer/id #uuid "ea75476f-d42e-58e7-be1d-885fe5dc9408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea75476f-d42e-58e7-be1d-885fe5dc9408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237291} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8f08e3-d8d9-5787-8e9c-79e2c433c62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01f0c95b-ef07-5cce-9a1b-7f84a8200390" :question/math-academy-id "q-10257" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=2{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{cos}^{3}⁡t)=-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{3}⁡t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d582ab-25c3-5d89-b31c-eee9f2c1897c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236147} {:answer/id #uuid "671a11ec-27d8-52b9-9a59-8dd0d0ec54c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236466} {:answer/id #uuid "7dedda59-24a2-5ab6-bb9d-20a20b1f41ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236599} {:answer/id #uuid "c654b5a5-a703-55fd-9759-75e1cbdb9a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237074} {:answer/id #uuid "e227f87d-b8bb-5a30-92ed-b9b3c38e19ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "671a11ec-27d8-52b9-9a59-8dd0d0ec54c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236466} :answer-field/id #uuid "58761fb6-2552-5c63-b655-9819d63debb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27c17b75-37ea-5768-beae-14242dea6c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-10258" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,y=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa28b6-42d5-5647-8c67-6ad4ae934fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235862} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb02d36-4654-5167-a760-32462c48caf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235991} {:answer/id #uuid "3ead8f61-e137-5573-914b-52260b377c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236214} {:answer/id #uuid "478c7a12-33ef-5729-9381-971f513d4e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236277} {:answer/id #uuid "c3466be1-0698-5115-8533-dbaa1ef80524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09aa28b6-42d5-5647-8c67-6ad4ae934fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235862} :answer-field/id #uuid "b04a5fcb-f5d2-5c81-be86-d5724d0bb948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31e80fe7-2790-57b6-8a22-f24ca436c4a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-10261" :question/problem "![](images/82/8288453c2625dac592a51054df332abf8ee49dbf8cbcabaf327c3ffe63b55f71.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+4-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =16{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166d0a6c-05a8-5786-b48c-f4e93b1f3b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235955} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc3551c-3d67-5cc6-8438-b49e16bdf115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236011} {:answer/id #uuid "45c69b36-6d72-503c-9c36-09fae5919dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236267} {:answer/id #uuid "d00d1ba1-3166-54b8-b47f-c8a1f0daadff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237136} {:answer/id #uuid "f55f988f-ed06-5427-b54b-471e782de7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45c69b36-6d72-503c-9c36-09fae5919dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236267} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f11615a-9ff4-5a1c-97b7-4c67c3c73fb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "440dd157-2982-5c1f-8661-23a8368ce029" :question/math-academy-id "q-102664" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=1-2t,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)^{2}(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1-x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1-x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1-x}{2}-2)}^{2}(\\frac{1-x}{2}+1) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1-x-4}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{1-x+2}{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-x-3}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{3-x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2663afa7-56a2-5ebd-ae11-a924299d9710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+5}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236061} {:answer/id #uuid "6395d88b-eecc-5b2d-9be3-8baa352e1b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-3}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236449} {:answer/id #uuid "80ccf35d-bc6a-5313-875a-745c0fe7950f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236619} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3ae7b-b354-5d77-a6b4-9ab7a0b0e1ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237341} {:answer/id #uuid "f45c9dd1-e74e-58da-9234-d134303089a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-5}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+3}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ccf35d-bc6a-5313-875a-745c0fe7950f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236619} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4533903-3cfe-53ab-953a-991cc8be771f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6c004f0-b68c-5924-ab03-701737917e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102682" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25757a6b-0934-5048-82f3-b1872f09625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236058} {:answer/id #uuid "7681af1b-9ec1-53a8-b458-886bf671a0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236559} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebad9c8-15e4-58ff-ad98-d9900ac0a3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236721} {:answer/id #uuid "bac9430a-dd6f-5e03-a05e-2e36c4335187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237012} {:answer/id #uuid "f9088ef8-1c5f-51c7-95f0-cb90fc5a2eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25757a6b-0934-5048-82f3-b1872f09625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236058} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d6b8ea-54a8-58e8-b6f4-45d73f4a2279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dadf2056-3a4c-505a-a071-2867e2e0c57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102760" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d5e141-e738-5b2a-a703-6da0a982dde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236350} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8ce46e-daf7-55c0-89df-337038a09ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236422} {:answer/id #uuid "68ed4719-9dea-5337-9667-8f3e5657f5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236480} {:answer/id #uuid "7f133e8a-78f1-5235-b7a3-a3e3ba78ba25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236604} {:answer/id #uuid "d944ae68-297d-5663-a79f-21d577d3bda0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d5e141-e738-5b2a-a703-6da0a982dde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236350} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a55434-2f0c-5beb-95aa-d1250e59d512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "962d8665-19cf-5669-bc0c-46b711cf96ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-102922" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12419a49-8a72-5d5a-b25b-542fc5dab0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235925} {:answer/id #uuid "4a01b9e0-c4f0-5704-a712-09144764bfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236292} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2e0013-e151-51e9-ad4a-bbb0f6be0b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237009} {:answer/id #uuid "c954ba9f-6430-5221-9f47-5bf333b81728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237094} {:answer/id #uuid "f813ee90-74dd-524e-9db4-b206cc8dc2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12419a49-8a72-5d5a-b25b-542fc5dab0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235925} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a48016-4413-5cfa-822b-c23d4158c7f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7720b821-7c21-51f2-9679-46c0e6df53a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-102941" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{4}{\\pi }{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{8}{\\pi }t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{\\pi }t)}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e539e8-4b94-5ebf-beb9-300464167207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235971} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc76372-7a9b-54d1-b0b5-2c5dc1bc0794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236006} {:answer/id #uuid "8f98c734-9d85-58c2-9226-5c0d06975e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236729} {:answer/id #uuid "cc413051-d394-5fa3-bd18-0b62ef6b4c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237114} {:answer/id #uuid "efe84d73-f1c3-5d70-8dbc-c95b7f7a755f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f98c734-9d85-58c2-9226-5c0d06975e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236729} :answer-field/id #uuid "709c36be-39f3-581f-94ab-d1e603723111" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "661ab230-8b4c-54fd-a081-ab2acd57768e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102993" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{3}}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{(t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{(1+1)^{2}}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t+1}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{2}{1+1}+K \\\\ 1 & =1+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f82c23-dce9-5d63-9fc6-34e72a047a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236056} {:answer/id #uuid "81f69b5d-b8d0-5e49-ab9e-1569317da21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236628} {:answer/id #uuid "979a582b-a8de-5112-b355-8ab7f8c9af85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236778} {:answer/id #uuid "e4bb209f-e09e-5bb4-a6dc-5604cdd3f64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237255} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4551c6-29d7-5e6b-9e9c-f0d16a2fdd95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81f69b5d-b8d0-5e49-ab9e-1569317da21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236628} :answer-field/id #uuid "2297541f-d883-54e0-b85f-33a2ccfd6866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1703b9b0-ea5e-5791-98c3-9b2cad2b5e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-102994" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=\\frac{1}{16}$ and velocity $v=\\frac{1}{4}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{1}{16}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & =0+C \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e29ce2-4554-5558-8c72-8422675e24c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236138} {:answer/id #uuid "3c164c19-1e2a-5b97-a1d5-77c725311763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236193} {:answer/id #uuid "66361318-9219-58c5-bcda-75129baaa714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236464} {:answer/id #uuid "8d01f3a1-7ccb-5a54-ae3f-83fd2498b7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236709} {:answer/id #uuid "b7289c68-237a-522e-b56e-57064d776048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7289c68-237a-522e-b56e-57064d776048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236980} :answer-field/id #uuid "88322035-f59b-5d15-97b6-79b1ff1b2397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "721c17b8-891a-5aa8-81e5-901c21b4cfea" :question/math-academy-id "q-103081" :question/problem "The derivative of $y=x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =1-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14927c29-0886-540d-95a2-4473de589ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235940} {:answer/id #uuid "42b67f48-9900-5969-bb84-e376aa0e142a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236244} {:answer/id #uuid "b71cb3ce-28e0-585f-9894-ebfd9f88d9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236978} {:answer/id #uuid "bae2f1a4-b12f-55b2-94e9-674f5ea009e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237013} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3be85b-3abb-5d86-a3c0-ca3e422113ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14927c29-0886-540d-95a2-4473de589ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235940} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f2a3ce-c6d5-5bd3-a262-29e4efe476df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c5a6195-b8ed-52d4-86b4-4695109267db" :question/math-academy-id "q-103279" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{3}{8},$ then $4cos⁡(x-10\\pi )+5=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x-10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{3}{8},$ then $cos⁡(x-10\\pi )=\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4cos⁡(x-10\\pi )+5 & =4(\\frac{3}{8})+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13daedb0-269b-5528-8bf1-ef0e7db6d1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235936} {:answer/id #uuid "28c4e15f-0467-5409-9d3f-33bb38a32677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236073} {:answer/id #uuid "49879257-8766-5452-b769-0d155fc10028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236286} {:answer/id #uuid "91eebbc5-a9e0-57e2-8f0e-fea041e01a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236744} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3b257a-4b42-5083-8400-b4b598baf86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13daedb0-269b-5528-8bf1-ef0e7db6d1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235936} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5d55f1-e6e1-538b-8968-4634effa5b5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "232fc51c-a784-5a0a-ab11-96190800b2c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-103307" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=\\frac{1}{3},$ for some value of $x,$ find the value of $12tan⁡(x+5\\pi )-6.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡x=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x=tan⁡(x+5\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x+5\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $tan⁡x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get that $tan⁡(x+5\\pi )=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n12tan⁡(x+5\\pi )-6=12(\\frac{1}{3})-6=4-6=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8ddc79-cd90-5a3a-92b9-66917add9af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236301} {:answer/id #uuid "84fa8218-25c6-5f9d-90b8-aac5aa59b9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236644} {:answer/id #uuid "928963ed-bc3c-5514-8870-62a00c67947b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236747} {:answer/id #uuid "b883f316-044b-5d72-a310-71831ab4f8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236997} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd85e1-113b-566e-9ea7-1f2d13a7d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd85e1-113b-566e-9ea7-1f2d13a7d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237027} :answer-field/id #uuid "250ff475-83b4-5e4f-9a44-f3c56416dc2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b89344e-126d-544e-88e0-0303a3eae594" :question/math-academy-id "q-103354" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35533677-024f-5015-b5fc-c9c35bcd97a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236155} {:answer/id #uuid "461eb43d-23b5-5be8-b5eb-d1535f26ab59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236269} {:answer/id #uuid "8c418cf2-df35-513d-86a0-953d17273ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236703} {:answer/id #uuid "c25d730c-740a-5089-a348-d56552dfbfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237047} {:answer/id #uuid "f31cf18d-2ca6-52a8-ae8f-44129d24abf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "461eb43d-23b5-5be8-b5eb-d1535f26ab59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236269} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae135409-b9c1-54f7-b3eb-0d0be03cf77d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "758e4600-77be-53f9-a21f-0c9a0a5da17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103440" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{-4-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(2,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb0cd4-c595-52e2-b7b8-f5921f1664ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235858} {:answer/id #uuid "339a3f8d-ec60-53c0-b70c-2ad1dcb8beef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236143} {:answer/id #uuid "bf918fbb-8ce0-57e4-ba33-761c02c1700f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237036} {:answer/id #uuid "cd49d0d7-034b-5674-9bb1-76d06ca38e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237119} {:answer/id #uuid "f90a028f-fc8e-54be-b155-96319e83f2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f90a028f-fc8e-54be-b155-96319e83f2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237390} :answer-field/id #uuid "be8d6f01-6ff4-56d4-9645-4daf3987bc75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2cb582-f805-50f4-ae14-8678782b90fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-103449" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+y & =-1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 3x+2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f907072-63f6-5f5f-8f48-b581ee8d9964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{5}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236017} {:answer/id #uuid "679b6c28-4249-5434-8796-946eedec1017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236471} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbc543c-735d-56bd-91ca-9de8c441f245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236814} {:answer/id #uuid "a80fa7f7-5d9d-58db-96e1-7ccc0ab7f96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{5}x-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236869} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f63932-6678-5b50-ad61-c13da4cdb606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{4}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679b6c28-4249-5434-8796-946eedec1017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236471} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87be670-f763-5d7b-8ef2-17d4e48d4561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4caa1a7-6407-5b10-be10-2977d4647c3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103455" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(8,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(8,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{5}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{5}{4}x-10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{4}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc92670-1ee0-5a94-8633-ae4dbac0d967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236503} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4214d-961d-587a-99f1-77840683d122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236888} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b8f9a0-77e0-5918-ba96-c076ccd0a4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237149} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b35714-a77b-5cf7-b620-bbbde6e0b91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237187} {:answer/id #uuid "f71cabe8-d7a8-53fa-97ce-f3e2f21c3251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4214d-961d-587a-99f1-77840683d122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236888} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fc67540-c6a4-5c72-ab19-c7578f38fabe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf6fcd75-6ae3-55a9-acc1-22b5660350be" :question/math-academy-id "q-103546" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(1,4)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ 2y-8 & =x-1 \\\\ x-2y & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=-7-(-2)=-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f53d0fb-f21f-5d85-9b8f-a7a4b136209c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236015} {:answer/id #uuid "2ebad11d-35cc-5957-b0cd-d331da650b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236113} {:answer/id #uuid "b02b6c4a-2dc7-5619-8048-51c5cb39616c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236936} {:answer/id #uuid "b628a64b-bad4-555c-8a56-2de7f107366f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236969} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8ca442-9f52-5b49-8b9a-0d2e18880c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{3}y=\\frac{x}{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f53d0fb-f21f-5d85-9b8f-a7a4b136209c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236015} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d740d7-f970-555c-93ca-f0630f56e033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9d6d25b-8b57-5b3f-a558-7b77221cef78" :question/math-academy-id "q-103551" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300b7028-8560-5889-820e-67bb7d82637c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{x}^{3}=\\sqrt{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236124} {:answer/id #uuid "424e1545-6d6e-5b8b-a579-6b2fe9723368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{e}^{xy}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236240} {:answer/id #uuid "4aad0d63-4d3a-5f79-90c3-39b4c6066d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236296} {:answer/id #uuid "4eeff264-a800-50f0-92db-506e7eed8ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236324} {:answer/id #uuid "f71e6f13-79fb-5eaa-9b80-7655ba53b105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aad0d63-4d3a-5f79-90c3-39b4c6066d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236296} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4d349a8-6f9f-579f-8e2e-6426ea734703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8586ab77-b8c5-52b0-a5f0-4361f0644199" :question/math-academy-id "q-103554" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc5000e-2b32-58d7-ad16-c7de786b85e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡(K|x|)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236225} {:answer/id #uuid "89e62549-2f57-5be7-80e7-7b01223e1dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(\\frac{K|x|}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236681} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca66147-f622-51fe-8f8d-c945ee0a90c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} {:answer/id #uuid "a162783a-2e70-5f31-8790-4cae86e77c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ln⁡(K|x|)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236827} {:answer/id #uuid "c42b65fc-6ad6-5255-ad37-78be530512d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ln⁡(2K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ca66147-f622-51fe-8f8d-c945ee0a90c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236797} :answer-field/id #uuid "684eb99d-9162-5ff2-afed-d23e9ffc201a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57af19c5-8aad-56d8-8498-0ea830a26c13" :question/math-academy-id "q-103603" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $2{x}^{-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2ln⁡|x|+2ln⁡K \\\\  & =2(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =2ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28be1daf-e4d6-5307-a254-ff0ec66dbf3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236072} {:answer/id #uuid "38818bd5-0d27-57c8-b3d8-774c7db2f12e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236175} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c44bc5-c614-5b30-bc98-b29666768cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236943} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7dd338-893e-506e-976e-d4705148656b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237021} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ff9fb-7a2e-5b19-897b-5bbe777d319b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38818bd5-0d27-57c8-b3d8-774c7db2f12e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236175} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c613f8-ed77-50c0-b40f-a68fbaeccb76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13ef05d7-e064-5d34-bef9-8301a2ad17b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-103658" :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-1-(-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7c9cd2-6944-5863-bc69-62975c2906f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235876} {:answer/id #uuid "98578c54-03fb-5056-afe0-ab2b99fb4ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236782} {:answer/id #uuid "ab22f6fd-a96c-5284-bc89-14c5b0fabbad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236898} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f243d1-8d1e-59c0-ba0c-0e8ecf122ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237190} {:answer/id #uuid "f855a1eb-9012-5b0e-946f-0d40498df3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98578c54-03fb-5056-afe0-ab2b99fb4ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236782} :answer-field/id #uuid "b49880a5-1e24-51a4-9758-7aac4e75c637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c44a1c94-220c-5631-9e8d-0fa005c996ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-103659" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})-(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546b362a-2747-5ca5-ab57-f8c2e8eaf565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236354} {:answer/id #uuid "92efcc88-d151-5156-8711-efffa00abe3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236750} {:answer/id #uuid "a24ccf86-225d-53ba-884b-3ce30a0309ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236835} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e0754e-5b71-56ae-8522-c2d740aa9e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237169} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1e377a-ed1e-5800-9161-b7da79f63267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92efcc88-d151-5156-8711-efffa00abe3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236750} :answer-field/id #uuid "62416cbe-aee9-592b-877e-f41f242d04f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "709039e0-3e6c-5c6c-99cd-c5947d7c381d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103668" :question/problem "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}+2x-2y-1=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =2y+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-1 & =2y+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2y+1+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2y+2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37094c1c-64ef-54f2-bffb-3cd777cefdc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236169} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9cf4d1-8065-5631-874e-1c1f2f07a91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236496} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca0da4e-fe77-563b-b779-c5885ae1769b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236796} {:answer/id #uuid "a39888c9-775b-5bd2-ae4d-602fc2cff52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236845} {:answer/id #uuid "c90ffe1d-a55c-5ad9-a58a-79b7d3fea6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a39888c9-775b-5bd2-ae4d-602fc2cff52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236845} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d73ce1f-6218-52e4-b2b1-bb911337c7dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb7e5ab4-e130-5607-b60f-25ef346d44f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103677" :question/problem "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $2{y}^{2}-x+4y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $y$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-x+4y & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+4y & =x \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}+2y] & =x \\\\ 2[(y+1)^{2}-1] & =x \\\\ 2(y+1)^{2}-2 & =x \\\\ 2(y+1)^{2} & =x+2 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{8})(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-2,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "678fd90a-32ab-579a-b9ba-0af076792792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236470} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b1ae8d-bde7-5e1d-904e-d1faee5cfcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236857} {:answer/id #uuid "abfa9785-b9a6-51ce-ace0-5967aa2b7eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236905} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fe9b65-c06d-5e06-84e1-c2f6acdd92bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237184} {:answer/id #uuid "f50fc346-f2e2-5c28-9c7e-fbbe0191df24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b1ae8d-bde7-5e1d-904e-d1faee5cfcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236857} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c73f8f-2066-5f09-9e77-36e9654a0ef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "241a4c6c-e9e1-5505-926c-873860bcd735" :question/math-academy-id "q-103772" :question/problem "Calculate $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ab5490-6958-5907-9744-daca5ba83c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-6{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235917} {:answer/id #uuid "23a1d755-1a8c-5412-a736-df2f34a61feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236046} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0f10c6-f01a-58b2-9e7c-e6f93eb8b719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+6{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236109} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5e3191-58c4-58b2-85d9-7d6953772bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236705} {:answer/id #uuid "96af5361-9440-53c2-b581-6ff64a3f4000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23a1d755-1a8c-5412-a736-df2f34a61feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236046} :answer-field/id #uuid "051b7d4c-b18a-50b8-837c-5476d5b85f2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "616e09b3-d157-50df-b328-51943da5bea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-103832" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))^{2}+(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e00f277-5143-5957-904d-a9dd31eb0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235892} {:answer/id #uuid "24bfddc8-84e0-588a-8616-05b13b833b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236054} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffac8cf-a6de-5f8b-b74d-5b6a9c38c64d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236123} {:answer/id #uuid "3673d5cc-88d2-50a8-a857-56a93c0d35f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236165} {:answer/id #uuid "5727ed7b-95e5-5433-8814-34b439a62973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5727ed7b-95e5-5433-8814-34b439a62973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236369} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe185be0-4aea-5d12-8405-2c55fa532bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aaa400a5-0216-5218-b483-3ceee85bd49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103834" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1bd01b4fe70a497cc66811a2d5b070908221b9932655ee396d9a8039471702c2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47084e96-4575-5ea2-bc29-d0119689a067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236272} {:answer/id #uuid "5c665951-8f9f-5315-b3f3-bac47dafdacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236396} {:answer/id #uuid "8127b6a0-4402-576d-a67a-897bdcc56f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236622} {:answer/id #uuid "d6287872-577f-5bdf-aa77-b5a797c91de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237171} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3ea81c-a5ed-52a6-801d-d9a3a71605f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47084e96-4575-5ea2-bc29-d0119689a067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236272} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca36830-c9ce-5e40-8c39-1b9f25663b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57b3d195-da37-5216-80d4-504f546a07a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-103837" :question/problem "![](images/33/3387790df770ac8645058c6f460b1f1285629d18d765668e362a8070ae4035ed.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above).\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10eb4957-edbb-5cdb-ac64-9bc72c43dea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235908} {:answer/id #uuid "acce3f64-38d4-545c-a90a-98ae08560f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236916} {:answer/id #uuid "d622bbba-38b9-58f1-aec8-b7200b12def3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237170} {:answer/id #uuid "e015dcfd-6510-5e73-8639-a91aea104895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237223} {:answer/id #uuid "fefc8cb9-5106-5ba6-8509-ba84de8e6560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acce3f64-38d4-545c-a90a-98ae08560f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236916} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bfbd4d3-1699-53a9-8de9-e66feb8a6c94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96931358-f8ea-56cc-a85a-c2b66216308c" :question/math-academy-id "q-103842" :question/problem "![](images/03/038d135bd0d8d163808fe21be0159a1a08db37c3e33599c97033ca0d53abab36.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268106be-19f1-5f82-813d-950192dc5718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236063} {:answer/id #uuid "a348d55a-36a9-5af2-81e6-6f7585bd95e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{9}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236841} {:answer/id #uuid "cf31be4e-0647-5297-be76-a4721eb22f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237129} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ca6f54-6773-56f6-8dd0-f043a63dc54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{9}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237197} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2a1ef9-6f90-5fd1-aa22-221a29a4b907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "268106be-19f1-5f82-813d-950192dc5718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236063} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e70a388-068c-5ad0-9ccf-1ecef596f218" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "072480e4-7dc0-5975-9c71-894af7eb1672" :question/math-academy-id "q-103890" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3x-y-2=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3x-y-2 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+3x)+({y}^{2}-y) & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =2+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {(x-(-\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1363c841-dd2f-57da-b97b-16bec04683d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},-2)$, radius: $\\frac{5}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235930} {:answer/id #uuid "47c89831-df18-53be-92f2-b52d6c8ba5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{5}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236279} {:answer/id #uuid "52ba3c55-6a2d-5c64-9e05-2892226192de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},-2)$, radius: $\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236342} {:answer/id #uuid "bf62ed9c-4b79-5d2c-83d9-7a2053919ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237033} {:answer/id #uuid "d0cd46be-6ca9-56ad-98b1-e84372745705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{3}{2},4)$, radius: $\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf62ed9c-4b79-5d2c-83d9-7a2053919ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$, radius: $\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237033} :answer-field/id #uuid "c35bda4d-8c7c-53e3-9565-e1df8075b9d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e504d2ff-bf2c-5a7b-89f3-0f34d5a7f90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103894" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3x-4y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3x-4y & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-3x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$ and its radius is $\\frac{5}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "921a2627-ec10-5d1e-8639-eef8fd7f3342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(0,-\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236745} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb55b6d-6427-54d7-925a-0d7d4383ae68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(0,\\frac{1}{4})$, radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236813} {:answer/id #uuid "b365be51-4d65-5928-9f90-0629516c1e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(0,-\\frac{1}{4})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236953} {:answer/id #uuid "b865178d-d257-5526-bfc0-ea89da1ca0eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(0,\\frac{1}{4})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236995} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d0e896-c4c4-58e2-a331-79ea27bb0deb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b865178d-d257-5526-bfc0-ea89da1ca0eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(0,\\frac{1}{4})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236995} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b4f34f-41d5-5588-8d90-691adc493613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6a657b9-6edf-5ed4-a2a2-a0a7062d8da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103896" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,\\frac{1}{4})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036e519f-c924-5649-b208-3cae45c9c3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238111} {:answer/id #uuid "60b2f004-1b0a-52b0-9b2b-ebdac2ed1485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238905} {:answer/id #uuid "8e197d1b-f46a-5bca-b996-28be90359d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239289} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7cf41d-bf13-5501-a648-617b7fb48f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239447} {:answer/id #uuid "c228a57c-3a58-5cc8-9d7b-d801859aadae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c228a57c-3a58-5cc8-9d7b-d801859aadae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239758} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c03836-aefa-5c2d-a5b3-6de207664cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed315823-9912-53f6-ae66-2685405a1454" :question/math-academy-id "q-103988" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)} & =\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+7=A(x+2)+B(2x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+7 & =A((-2)+2)+B(2(-2)+1) \\\\ 3 & =-3B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+7 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}+2)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 6 & =A(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)} & =\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2402a50b-0ada-5a95-bd1c-d60ffe3a7054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238371} {:answer/id #uuid "3113425a-30f8-5c8b-88b3-34e913ccf225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238481} {:answer/id #uuid "79eaa63a-7157-5816-ab3b-72d58acb2790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239106} {:answer/id #uuid "f7406111-8e32-5b08-aa41-0f61204a9031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240235} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71d029-d6be-50c5-9b70-7d320b3399c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd71d029-d6be-50c5-9b70-7d320b3399c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240297} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1159eaa-cd25-57b9-b6f3-0999eae2bb5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1086559f-da78-5256-be5a-8d4d9777777a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104055" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-x-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-0-C \\\\ 0 & =0-C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-x\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157a8223-0c9b-5529-8733-655ea9476c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238233} {:answer/id #uuid "2412dd69-dc1d-5afb-b423-8cac607f549c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238372} {:answer/id #uuid "b0aa80ef-c925-5891-9d12-dd7bc86a59a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239590} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ebce3a-e306-509f-97b8-f30222fa0ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239830} {:answer/id #uuid "f47fc246-ebae-5589-8afa-6aa1ff9c0ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2412dd69-dc1d-5afb-b423-8cac607f549c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238372} :answer-field/id #uuid "601a065b-da49-5c92-b360-c74641979347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33ee86bf-83f2-58e4-8a42-08be20a94a04" :question/math-academy-id "q-10406" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75be7c90-32ab-5c64-906d-1dd7954d2121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239071} {:answer/id #uuid "82693f98-0710-5635-895b-2a14d84f4e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239189} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7da46f-6bf6-5179-ad99-a6c9aa84ab9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8{x}^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239852} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ba6483-6e8b-5e55-93bc-ce43b1b86246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+8{x}^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240204} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c87060-4f09-5b9a-ba36-024d4f3b3691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75be7c90-32ab-5c64-906d-1dd7954d2121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239071} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9d1e6a-7b24-50e3-9ef5-4dad3d944987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdddaa9b-4174-550d-a7b6-1b8e1a3f2b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-104065" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that satisfies $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}.$ Assume that $0<x<\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+4{x}^{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral on the right-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{C}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}+{C}_{2}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{\\pi }^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that satisfies the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\pi }^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25865f2a-e9ab-502a-9acf-7363a0aeafd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238382} {:answer/id #uuid "36b1af99-ddb4-5cb0-a70e-0df377a0803c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238527} {:answer/id #uuid "56768848-d4e4-5fe2-9bc0-bb95caa15190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238801} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d0920a-c212-57be-bc60-2bc222f3ffe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240085} {:answer/id #uuid "ee40857a-913f-5d4e-b282-482d4d4ed99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee40857a-913f-5d4e-b282-482d4d4ed99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240153} :answer-field/id #uuid "197a188d-df31-5b68-9f39-274a3ad96820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab4c66af-99f0-54ce-8f3e-d6fc71302f94" :question/math-academy-id "q-104119" :question/problem "Simplify the expression ${[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})]}^{20}.$" :question/worked-solution "De Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=20$ and $\\phi =\\frac{2\\theta }{5}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{5})]}^{20} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\cdot \\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\cdot \\frac{2\\theta }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43abf844-b27d-518a-86b4-213fdf37b875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238641} {:answer/id #uuid "6e01f608-86ec-5f3a-8fbc-be42293384a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239001} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7b6159-a77f-5723-93fb-678335739c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239428} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1c356f-fd3c-54d8-93d8-3f2ac109eefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239566} {:answer/id #uuid "b1067e17-6cec-5d26-b4f5-5f89de8394e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2[\\operatorna